{"name":"Simmering Smoke","hex":"#a99f96","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"simmering","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of simmer"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of something that simmers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/simmering"},{"term":"smoke","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible vapor/vapour, gases, and fine particles given off by burning or smoldering material.","A cigarette."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To inhale and exhale the smoke from a burning cigarette or cigar, burning material in a pipe, etc.","To inhale and exhale tobacco smoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoke"}],"entries":[{"title":"Simmering","blocks":[{"text":"Simmering is a food preparation technique by which foods are cooked in hot liquids kept just below the boiling point of water and above poaching temperature. To create a steady simmer, a liquid is brought to a boil, then its heat source is reduced to a lower, constant intensity. While simmering, a liquid will show little movement without approaching a rolling boil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/SopaAlbondigasPot.JPG/330px-SopaAlbondigasPot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simmering","wikidata":"Q240164"},{"title":"Smoke","blocks":[{"text":"Smoke is an aerosol emitted when a material undergoes combustion or pyrolysis, together with the quantity of air that is entrained or otherwise mixed into the mass. It is commonly an unwanted by-product of fires, but may also be used for pest control (fumigation), communication, defensive and offensive capabilities in the military, cooking, or smoking. It is used in rituals where incense, sage, or resin is burned to produce a smell for spiritual or magical purposes. It can also be a flavoring agent and preservative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg/330px-Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoke","wikidata":"Q130768"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Molen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg/500px-Molen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Molen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg/1280px-Molen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Molen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg/500px-Molen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical Dutch burner for simmering meat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMolen_Emmamolen_Nieuwkuijk%2C_petroleumstel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg/500px-Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg/500px-Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chemical composition distribution of volatile organic compounds released in smoke from a variety of solid fuels[4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASmoke_chemical_composition.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Diesel-smoke.jpg/330px-Diesel-smoke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Diesel-smoke.jpg/330px-Diesel-smoke.jpg?utm_sourc